WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.989
Let's start with what you're looking at.

00:00:02.939 --> 00:00:09.203
This is a close-up model of water with sugar dissolved in it, like sugar stirred into a drink.

00:00:09.203 --> 00:00:13.369
The small blue balls represent water molecules.

00:00:13.369 --> 00:00:16.731
The larger gold balls represent sugar.

00:00:16.731 --> 00:00:21.467
They're drawn much bigger than real particles, so we can see them.

00:00:21.467 --> 00:00:25.606
Dilute means less dissolved sugar in the same volume.

00:00:25.606 --> 00:00:28.217
Concentrated means more.

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:37.139
The grey barrier in the middle represents a membrane: a thin boundary, like the membrane around a living cell.

00:00:37.089 --> 00:00:40.451
Think of a filter with tiny openings.

00:00:40.451 --> 00:00:46.951
In our model, the blue water particles can get through, but the gold sugar particles cannot.

00:00:46.951 --> 00:00:49.923
We call this partially permeable.

00:00:49.923 --> 00:00:53.381
Watch the blue particles cross the boundary.

00:00:53.381 --> 00:00:58.951
Osmosis is about water crossing this kind of membrane, not sugar crossing it.

00:01:00.550 --> 00:01:03.508
Now compare the two sides.

00:01:03.458 --> 00:01:08.028
The left has less sugar per volume, so it is more dilute.

00:01:08.028 --> 00:01:12.236
The right has more sugar, so it is more concentrated.

00:01:12.236 --> 00:01:16.264
Water particles are constantly moving randomly.

00:01:16.264 --> 00:01:24.056
They cross in both directions, but overall, more water moves from the dilute side to the concentrated side.

00:01:24.056 --> 00:01:28.778
The bigger arrow shows this overall, or net, movement.

00:01:28.778 --> 00:01:31.097
That is osmosis.

00:01:31.097 --> 00:01:37.778
The water is not choosing where to go, and the cell does not spend energy from respiration to move it.

00:01:39.390 --> 00:01:43.418
Here, the solutions have equal concentrations.

00:01:43.368 --> 00:01:46.368
Keep watching the blue particles.

00:01:46.368 --> 00:01:49.298
Water has not stopped moving.

00:01:49.298 --> 00:01:54.896
It still crosses both ways, but the opposing flows balance out overall.

00:01:54.896 --> 00:01:58.118
That means no net movement of water.

00:01:58.118 --> 00:02:05.840
Think of equal numbers of people entering and leaving a room: people are moving, but the number in the room stays the same.

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:09.048
This is what we mean by equilibrium.

00:02:10.670 --> 00:02:15.073
The left shape is a plant cell, with a strong outer wall.

00:02:15.023 --> 00:02:18.842
The right is an animal cell, without that wall.

00:02:18.842 --> 00:02:26.537
If the surrounding solution is more dilute than the cell contents, water enters through the cell membrane by osmosis.

00:02:26.537 --> 00:02:32.148
The plant cell becomes firm, or turgid, because its wall resists expansion.

00:02:32.148 --> 00:02:35.606
An animal cell can swell and may burst.

00:02:35.606 --> 00:02:41.773
This explains why water entering potato cells can make a potato piece gain mass.

00:02:43.390 --> 00:02:49.071
Now imagine the solution outside a cell is more concentrated than its contents.

00:02:49.021 --> 00:02:54.437
Before the answer appears, predict: will water enter the cell or leave it?

00:02:54.437 --> 00:02:57.632
Pause the video if you need more time.

00:02:57.632 --> 00:03:02.882
Water leaves overall, through the partially permeable cell membrane.

00:03:02.882 --> 00:03:06.007
The cell loses water and shrinks.

00:03:06.007 --> 00:03:09.173
A potato piece would lose mass.

00:03:09.173 --> 00:03:18.646
Remember: osmosis is the net movement of water from a dilute solution to a more concentrated solution through a partially permeable membrane.

